def rep(p,a,b):
    s=open(p).read(); assert s.count(a)==1,(p,a[:70]); open(p,'w').write(s.replace(a,b))
lines=open('CHANGELOG.md').read().split('\n')
assert lines[6].startswith('- Guards judge a Bash command whose head word is a shell alias')
lines[6]=("- Guards judge a Bash command whose head word is a shell alias from the Claude Code shell snapshot, such as `gpf! origin main`, as the git or gh command it expands to, as well as itself. "
"With aliases loaded, the guards read the command's own simple commands exactly as without aliases, in the same order and context, then every expansion after all of them, so an alias can only add votes: it never removes a deny or an ask, and never gets an allow, which kern gives only to a command that starts with git or gh. "
"The head word is found after assignments (quoted ones included), redirections and zsh's reserved words (`if`, `!`, `time` and the like), in each simple command, `$( )` and `eval`. "
"An alias whose value ends in a space, such as `_='sudo '`, also expands the word after it, as zsh's trailing-space rule does, so `_ gpf! origin main` is judged as `sudo git push --force origin main`; past 8 such words, a word that is still an alias is denied under autopilot. "
"G2, G3, G4, G13, G14 and G15, which skip a command whose text names no gh, push, deploy tool or network client, read the expansion's text too, so `prc` for `gh pr create`, `gp origin production` as a push to a deploy branch, and an alias that runs psql against a protected target are judged. "
"kern reads the snapshot as text and never runs it; a missing, unreadable or unparseable snapshot changes nothing. This removes the 0.2.0 known limit on shell aliases. "
"Known limits, left for M3: the newest snapshot stands in for the session's, which the hook input does not name; an alias defined and used in one Bash call through `eval`, `$( )` or a backtick is not judged interactively (G00 denies the definition under autopilot); nor is an alias past another alias's head word, a global alias, a `$'...'` or double-quoted alias value, a chain of aliases deeper than 8, or interactively an alias past 8 trailing-space words; an expansion no longer sets context for the command's later records, so `gaa && git commit -m x` loses G6's stale-index reason (its decision stays); a snapshot with thousands of aliases slows every Bash call, by about 0.6 s for 10,000; G16 and G20 match only the command's own text, not an alias's expansion; and the shell-snapshots folder is writable, so kern reads a newer snapshot written there, though that can only add votes, never remove a deny or an ask or add an allow.")
open('CHANGELOG.md','w').write('\n'.join(lines))
c='docs/plans/contract.md'
rep(c,"- `git_words` (M2a Task 33): a record whose head word is an alias `aliases.sh` loaded is printed, then its expansion is split again and printed after it; the expansion's own words are not expanded again. The record stays so that no vote the command got without aliases is lost, and so that a guard that allows only a command whose first record starts with git or gh never allows an alias. A quoted or escaped head word is never an alias. Without `aliases.sh` loaded, nothing changes.",
"- `git_words` (M2a Task 33): with aliases loaded (`KERN_ALIASES` set), the outermost call prints the command's own records exactly as without aliases (content, order and context), then every expansion record after all of them. An inner call splits a record's alias expansion again (`_GW_NOALIAS` set, so its own words are not expanded again) and marks each of its records with a leading tab, which no record of the command starts with; the outermost call moves the marked records to the end and strips the mark. So an expansion never changes the context of a record of the command (a `cd` in it cannot clear G1's or G6's `moved` or G16's `in_sd`), and expansions can only add votes. The alias record itself stays first, so a guard that allows only a command whose first record starts with git or gh never allows an alias. A quoted or escaped head word is never an alias. `_GW_IN` and `_GW_NOALIAS` are unset when `gitctx.sh` is sourced. Without `aliases.sh` loaded, nothing changes.")
rep(c,"and of each alias whose expansion ends in a blank (stored with one space at its end)","and of each alias whose expansion ends in a space (a tab does not count, as in zsh; stored with one space at its end)")
rep(c,"- `alias_expand SEG`: sets `ALIAS_EXP` to SEG with its head word replaced; exit 1 when that word is not such an alias. The head word is the first word after unquoted `NAME=value` words, redirections (with a separate target word) and zsh's reserved words `if then elif else do while until ! { time nocorrect coproc` and `repeat` with its count, as zsh finds it; not after `command`, `builtin`, `exec`, `noglob` or `-`, where zsh does not expand. When the expansion ends in a blank, the word right after the alias is replaced too when it is such an alias, as zsh's trailing-space rule does, at most 8 times.",
"- `alias_expand SEG`: sets `ALIAS_EXP` to SEG with its head word replaced; exit 1 when that word is not such an alias. The head word is the first word after `NAME=value` words, redirections (with a separate target word) and zsh's reserved words `if then elif else do while until ! { time nocorrect coproc` and `repeat` with its count, as zsh finds it; not after `command`, `builtin`, `exec`, `noglob` or `-`, where zsh does not expand. An assignment word holding a quote, `$` or a backslash hands the search to `_ALIAS_HEAD_AWK`, which splits words at blanks outside quotes, `$( )`, `${ }` and backticks. When the expansion ends in a space, the word right after the alias is replaced too when it is such an alias, as zsh's trailing-space rule does, 8 times; a 9th word that is still such an alias becomes the command name `$KERN_ALIAS_PAST_8`, which G00 reads as built at run time (deny under autopilot; no vote interactively, as without aliases).")
rep(c,"Not followed: aliases inside an expansion other than its head-word chain, a run of trailing-space aliases past 8, global aliases, an alias after an assignment word holding a quote or `$`, and an alias defined in the same Bash call and run through `eval` or `$( )` (G00 denies an alias definition under autopilot).",
"Not followed: aliases inside an expansion other than its head-word chain, global aliases, and an alias defined in the same Bash call and run through `eval`, `$( )` or a backtick (G00 denies an alias definition under autopilot). Over-expansion, harmless because expansions only add votes: G16 runs `git_words` on the lowercased command, so its lookup can find an alias zsh would not run (`gbD` is looked up as `gbd`); and an alias is expanded inside `bash -c`, `sh -c` and ssh remote command text too, where the snapshot's aliases are not defined. An expansion sets no context for the command's own later records: `gaa && git commit -m x` loses G6's stale-index reason, though the decision is unchanged.")
